findings.body:
The helper is the entry point for the new Studies and Rollouts text, but `formatStudiesText` formats `strings.detailTextStudies`, the v1 "may install and run studies" description. The new wording for the split is `detailTextStudiesV2`. `formatRolloutsText` correctly uses its new key, so the two methods are inconsistent.

Nothing calls these methods today, so no user sees this yet. The follow-up split will likely wire the Studies toggle to this helper, and that would ship the old copy.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-430"}

::callout{type="warning"}
`SettingsViewController.swift` (lines 127-128) still needs `detailTextStudies`, so the legacy constant must stay. Only the helper should change.

The five new Focus constants (`detailTextSendUsageDataV2`, `labelStudiesV2`, `detailTextStudiesV2`, `labelRollouts`, `detailTextRollouts`) and the `SettingsTextFormatter` singleton are not referenced anywhere in the repo. Staging copy ahead of the split is expected, so the constants are fine to keep.

The helper is different. It is an abstraction with no consumer and no test, and it duplicates what `SettingsViewController` already does inline with `String(format:)` at lines 127-130. Any bug in it, like the wrong key above, will go unnoticed until someone wires it in.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-430"}

The new exclusion `firefox-ios.xliff:Settings.Studies.Message.v148` suppresses the quotes check for a key whose English value is `%@ randomly selects users to test features, which improves quality for everyone.` That value contains no quote characters, so the exclusion either does nothing or covers an issue in a translation or xliff that this repo does not show.

The PR description does not explain the exclusion. Excluding by key also hides any future quote problem in that message, so it needs a reason on record.

